<http://purl.org/nanopub/temp/np1/assertion> {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/maynardsmith-twofold-cost> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/maynardsmith-twofold-cost> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Twofold cost of sex" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/maynardsmith-twofold-cost> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "An asexual mutant female in a sexual population with equal sex ratio produces twice as many grandchildren as a sexual female, so sex needs a compensating advantage of up to twofold to persist." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/maynardsmith-twofold-cost> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/TestableHypothesis> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/maynardsmith-twofold-cost> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "with equal fecundity and a 1:1 sex ratio, the odds of asexual to sexual females double each generation: \\frac{q_{t+1}}{1-q_{t+1}} = 2\\frac{q_t}{1-q_t}" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/maynardsmith-twofold-cost> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Assumptions: anisogamy with males contributing no resources, equal fecundity and viability of sexual and asexual females, a 1:1 sex ratio. Formal status: proved as a population-dynamic identity under these assumptions. Its biological force depends on how often those assumptions hold." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/maynardsmith-twofold-cost> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/proposedTest> "In a species with coexisting sexual and asexual lineages, measure relative per-capita growth of asexual versus sexual females in a common environment and compare it with the predicted factor of 2." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/maynardsmith-twofold-cost> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hypothesisType> "model-theorem" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/maynardsmith-twofold-cost> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "John Maynard Smith (1978) The Evolution of Sex" .
}
